WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.459
If you’ve ventured inside a convenience
store recently, you might have noticed signs

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:09.290
asking for exact change only, or even offering
to buy your change.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:14.180
That’s because America is currently undergoing
a coin shortage, or more accurately,

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.580
a “circulation disruption.”

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.329
Thanks to COVID-19, people aren’t using
as much cash, and aren’t redeeming coins

00:00:21.329 --> 00:00:24.449
at places like banks and grocery stores.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:30.289
That means a lot of coins just sitting in
homes in jars and piggy banks, not being used.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:35.220
That may not sound like a big deal if you
buy everything online, but millions of “unbanked”

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.250
Americans rely on cash to get by, and many
services, like coin-operated laundromats,

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.140
have no other payment options.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:46.810
This is likely to persist until people start
using coins freely again, so we thought it

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:51.870
was the perfect time to premiere this episode
we shot before the pandemic struck about the

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.460
importance of your loose change.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.730
Enjoy!

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.730
If you’ve ever used a five dollar bill to
buy something that costs $4 and one cent,

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:04.930
you might have felt conflicted about that
handful of coins you received in change.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.860
Coins that will either be jangling in your
pocket all day, cluttering up your car or

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.980
slipping between your sofa cushions.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:17.110
You know it’s money--it’s worth something--but
maybe less than the trouble of holding onto

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.110
it.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:21.530
How did we get to the point where legal tender
could be considered a burden?

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.960
Will things ever change?

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.600
And until then, what should you do with that
change?

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.360
You’re probably aware that pennies didn’t
start out worthless.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.090
In 1913, they had roughly the purchasing power
of today’s quarters.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.460
A handful of them could’ve bought you a
quart of milk or a bite to eat.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.830
But after a hundred years of inflation, today’s
pennies and nickels are worth less than it

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.420
costs to make them!

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.360
No rational government would intentionally
start minting coins with so little value,

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:58.680
yet we seem to be stuck with them for the
time being.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.020
Why is that?

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:05.020
Again, inflation is one of the culprits--or
rather, the fear of inflation.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.310
Thanks to America’s weird pricing practices,
it’s common to see numbers like these at

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.310
checkout.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.400
Many people assume that if we eliminate the
penny, retailers will round up their prices

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.550
to the nearest nickel.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:21.640
Doesn’t seem like such a big deal when you’re
grabbing a soda from 7-11, but some economists

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.650
fear that if all the prices on all the goods
across the whole country go up at the same

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.800
time, it could kickstart a spike in inflation.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.730
Others have conducted studies that suggest
this fear is overblown.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.810
Sentimentality is a much more likely reason.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.520
Most Americans are against eliminating the
penny, and 43% said they’d be “disappointed”

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.530
or “angry” if they were discontinued.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:48.400
Without overwhelming popular support, it’s
unlikely that Congress will abolish such an

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.599
iconic element of American history, so U.S.
mints continue to roll out around 10 billion

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:59.100
pennies a year, more than all the other coins
combined.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.200
Why so many?

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:06.610
In part, because so many disappear--into couch
cushions, junk drawers, fountains, and landfills.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.930
Americans throw away over $60 million worth
of coins every year!

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:16.820
11% of people said they’d rather throw a
penny into the trash than hang on to it.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:18.319
Don’t do that.

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:21.959
I know a penny isn’t worth much, but there
are far better places to put them.

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:22.959
Like piggy banks!

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:26.560
Not to get off on a tangent, but what’s
the deal with piggy banks anyway?

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.599
It’s not like pigs are known for their frugality
and restraint, so what gives?

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:35.270
Thank you for that spontaneous and not-at-all
pre-planned question!

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:39.330
The origins of the piggy bank are unclear,
but the dominant theory is that in the Middle

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:44.150
Ages, people would keep coins in jars made
out of a clay called “pygg”.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:49.709
Over the next few hundred years, the vowel
sound shifted from uh to ih, making the word

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:52.660
similar to the Middle English word for swine,
“pigge.”

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:58.409
By the 18th century, potters were making pygg
jars in the shape of pigs as a kind of visual

00:03:58.409 --> 00:04:01.129
pun, and boy did it catch on.

00:04:01.129 --> 00:04:05.209
Unfortunately, not many of these original
piggy banks are around today, because you

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:08.350
have to, y’know, smash ‘em to get your
money out.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:13.360
However, there is a surviving cousin of the
piggy bank: a clay vessel filled with hot

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.000
water that people still use in certain places
to warm their beds, sometimes called a “pig.”

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.200
So once your piggy bank is full, what should
you do with all those pennies, nickels and dimes?

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.440
Unless you want to be the most unpopular person
at your supermarket, we don’t recommend

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.140
using them for big purchases.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:35.010
Instead, you could pour them into one of those
machines they often have near the entrance,

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:40.340
which automatically sift and sort your change
and print out a receipt you can redeem with the cashier.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.680
The catch?

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.880
They typically charge a processing fee over
10%, which is kind of a lot to turn money

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:49.110
into… money.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.250
Most coin machines will allow you to skip
the fee by putting the value on a gift card

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.170
instead--which is great if it’s for a place
where you know you’ll spend anyway.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:01.650
They also allow you to automatically donate
the money to one of several charities--which

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:05.150
has got to be one of the most painless ways
to be generous.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:08.180
Just make sure to keep that receipt for your
taxes!

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:12.540
Maybe you’d prefer to try your local bank,
but be warned: the federal government does

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.160
not require banks to accept coins.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.560
It’s totally up to the institution, and
will often depend on whether you have an account

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.730
there, whether the coins are rolled, and even
how busy they are--and many banks are starting

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.400
to charge fees for processing coins.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.009
It’s best to call ahead before you load
those buckets into the trunk of your car.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:35.500
Perhaps the best use for loose change is giving
it to children!

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:39.970
It’s worth a lot more to them than it is
to adults, it can be used as a reward for

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:44.860
desirable behavior, teach them about math
and economics, and introduce them to the pride

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.669
of saving when it’s finally redeemed.

00:05:47.669 --> 00:05:51.810
Just remember to keep a little change for
yourself, because one of the best uses for

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:54.660
it… is to avoid collecting more!

00:05:54.660 --> 00:06:00.430
With just 1 nickel, 2 dimes, 3 quarters and
4 pennies, you can nail any number that comes

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:01.640
after that decimal point.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:06.830
So if you don’t want to accumulate any more
change than you already have, just grab 1

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.960
2 3 4 on your way out the door.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:15.080
In the era of credit cards and payment apps,
it’s easy to start seeing loose change as

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.780
worthless, but there’s a danger to this
way of thinking.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:24.000
If you start internalizing the idea that a
$1.55 bottle of soda costs $2 in cash, you’re

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:27.710
basically acquiescing to a 30% price increase.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:30.699
Over thousands of transactions, that can add
up.

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:35.770
If that’s not enough motivation, consider
using those savings to treat yourself to something

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:37.100
fun or frivolous.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.760
Since we tend to think of loose change as
“valueless,” there’s less guilt in spending it.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:46.220
It doesn’t look like pennies and nickels
are going anywhere for a while, so whether

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:51.900
you put it in a piggy bank, donate it to charity
or give it to your kids as an allowance, anything’s

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.470
better than throwing it away.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:58.990
But maybe get one of those piggy banks with
the stopper, just in case you get too emotionally

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.360
attached to send 
him to hog heaven.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:02.880
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.120
